---
import CompassDial from './CompassDial.astro';
import PhoneShell from './PhoneShell.astro';
---
<section class="hero" id="vfHero">
  <div class="hero__grid" id="vfHeroGrid" aria-hidden="true">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 1200 800" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" focusable="false">
      <g stroke="#D3D6C9" stroke-width="1">
        <line x1="0" y1="200" x2="1200" y2="200" />
        <line x1="0" y1="400" x2="1200" y2="400" />
        <line x1="0" y1="600" x2="1200" y2="600" />
        <line x1="300" y1="0" x2="300" y2="800" />
        <line x1="600" y1="0" x2="600" y2="800" />
        <line x1="900" y1="0" x2="900" y2="800" />
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <div class="hero__dial" id="vfHeroDial" aria-hidden="true">
    <CompassDial variant="hero" />
  </div>

  <div class="hero__cols">
    <div>
      {/* "India's No. 1 Vastu App" is gone. The app has not launched, so there is no ranking
          it could be first in — and this site's whole argument is that our advice can be traced
          to a source. Opening on a claim nobody can check undercuts every honest line below it. */}
      <h1 class="h1" data-reveal="1">Read your home’s<br /><em>Vastu</em></h1>

      {/* ⚠ "before a single brick is laid" described the ONE person in three the app is not
          mostly for. Its own first screen leads with "I am buying a home", because most people
          open it holding a plan somebody else has already built. The sentence now covers what it
          actually does, and says house-or-flat, which became true in v0.22.0. */}
      {/* ⚠ "You check every room before anything is scored" stopped being true in app v0.35.0,
          when the separate checking screen went. The owner's line replaces it (30 Sep 2026), here,
          in "How it works" and in the page's description. */}
      <p class="hero__sub" data-reveal="2">
        Photograph your floor plan and the app reads the rooms off it — or draw one by hand. You
        can correct any room before your score. House or flat, and every rule names where it
        comes from.
      </p>

      <div class="hero__actions" data-reveal="3">
        {/* The two badges live in their own grid so they are exactly the same
            width. Left to themselves they size to their own text and glyph,
            and "Google Play" is wider than "App Store". */}
        {/* ⛔ THE APP STORE BADGE IS GONE. iPhone is not a decision the owner has made — not
            "later", not "after Android", not decided. A store badge is the strongest promise a
            landing page can make, and this one was promising a product nobody has agreed to build.
            The grid keeps its two-column rule and simply has one child, which is what it already
            does at narrow widths. */}
        <div class="store-badges">
        <div class="store-badge">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" style="width:24px;height:24px;flex:0 0 24px" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M4 3.5 L15.5 12 L4 20.5 Z" fill="none" stroke="#F8F6F0" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" />
            <path d="M15.5 12 L19.5 9.4 L21 12 L19.5 14.6 Z" fill="#C9A227" />
          </svg>
          <div>
            <div class="store-badge__over">Coming soon on</div>
            <div class="store-badge__name">Google Play</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        </div>

        <a class="text-link" href="/#vfNotify" data-jump="vfNotify">Get launch access →</a>
      </div>

      <p class="meta-row" data-reveal="4">
        <span>81-pada grid</span>
        <span class="meta-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>Every rule sourced</span>
        <span class="meta-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>No sign-up</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="hero__phone-cell" data-reveal="2">
      <div class="float-wrap" id="vfFloatWrap">
        <PhoneShell>
          {/* ⚠ THIS MOCK SHOWED A SCREEN THE APP DOES NOT HAVE. Three inventions in four lines:
              a "Step 2 of 3" counter, which the app deliberately REMOVED because it was wrong on
              every path (a scanned home is five screens, a drawn one is four); a heading reading
              "Face the road, hold steady"; and a "Lock north" button. The real screen asks "Which
              way is North?", explains the dial, and its button reads "Read my home".

              The three screens under "How it works" are the app's own recorded screenshots for
              exactly this reason. This one is hand-built HTML because the compass animates on
              scroll — so its WORDS have to be checked against the app by hand. They now match. */}
          <div class="mn__head">
            <div class="phone__step">Mark north</div>
            <div class="phone__title">Which way<br />is North?</div>
            <div class="mn__deg">
              <span class="mn__deg-num" id="vfDeg">0°</span>
              <span class="mn__deg-dir" id="vfDir">N</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="mn__dial">
            <CompassDial variant="phone" groupId="vfPhoneDial" />
          </div>

          <div class="phone__cta">Read my home</div>
        </PhoneShell>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="scroll-hint" aria-hidden="true">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 22 30" style="width:22px;height:30px" focusable="false">
      <rect x="1" y="1" width="20" height="28" rx="10" fill="none" stroke="#98A08C" stroke-width="1.2" />
      <circle cx="11" cy="9" r="2.4" fill="#C9A227" />
    </svg>
  </div>
</section>
